00:08:12.790 --> 00:08:15.610
ALEX DUFETEL: So Cloud Firestore
is a fully managed NoSQL

00:08:15.610 --> 00:08:16.960
database.

00:08:16.960 --> 00:08:19.060
It comes as the second
database offering

00:08:19.060 --> 00:08:21.250
in the Firebase portfolio.

00:08:21.250 --> 00:08:22.974
So users will now
have two databases

00:08:22.974 --> 00:08:25.390
that they can choose from, the
Realtime Database and Cloud

00:08:25.390 --> 00:08:26.175
Firestore.

00:08:26.175 --> 00:08:27.550
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
So you mentioned

00:08:27.550 --> 00:08:29.470
it's a non-relational
database, which

00:08:29.470 --> 00:08:31.660
makes me think about Bigtable.

00:08:31.660 --> 00:08:35.169
But I'm also assuming that
it has all the goodness

00:08:35.169 --> 00:08:37.539
from the Realtime
Database, so it's still

00:08:37.539 --> 00:08:39.947
that real-time nature of it.

00:08:39.947 --> 00:08:40.780
ALEX DUFETEL: Right.

00:08:40.780 --> 00:08:43.720
So stepping back a little
bit, Cloud Firestore

00:08:43.720 --> 00:08:47.070
addresses many of the
limitations of the Realtime

00:08:47.070 --> 00:08:47.910
Database.

00:08:47.910 --> 00:08:50.350
The Realtime Database has
long been the flagship product

00:08:50.350 --> 00:08:51.950
of Firebase.

00:08:51.950 --> 00:08:56.260
It was among its first
offering of products,

00:08:56.260 --> 00:09:00.000
and it solved many, many hard
problems that developers have.

00:09:00.000 --> 00:09:04.900
One, it makes it very easy to
imagine and scale your backend.

00:09:04.900 --> 00:09:08.260
And it allows developers to
power real-time features,

00:09:08.260 --> 00:09:11.320
which are, without proper
tooling, very difficult

00:09:11.320 --> 00:09:12.040
to build.

00:09:12.040 --> 00:09:15.040
And it also allows for--

00:09:15.040 --> 00:09:17.440
to build offline ready apps.

00:09:17.440 --> 00:09:20.950
The problems with the Realtime
Database is that as we scale--

00:09:20.950 --> 00:09:25.360
the Realtime Database is
a very successful product

00:09:25.360 --> 00:09:28.250
with hundreds of thousands
developers using it.

00:09:28.250 --> 00:09:30.850
But as the database
scaled, and as we've

00:09:30.850 --> 00:09:34.030
started having more usage,
usage patterns evolved.

00:09:34.030 --> 00:09:37.150
And what we saw is people
are using the database

00:09:37.150 --> 00:09:39.620
across all their app needs,
not just the real-time bits.

00:09:39.620 --> 00:09:40.930
And as they were
doing so, they really

00:09:40.930 --> 00:09:42.346
hit two different
types of limits.

00:09:42.346 --> 00:09:47.380
The first is around data
structuring and querying,

00:09:47.380 --> 00:09:50.075
and the other is
around scalability.

00:09:50.075 --> 00:09:51.700
What happens with
the Realtime Database

00:09:51.700 --> 00:09:55.990
is that if you want to build
very sophisticated apps

00:09:55.990 --> 00:09:58.540
and access your data in
very sophisticated ways,

00:09:58.540 --> 00:10:01.810
you sometimes have to structure
it in convoluted ways.

00:10:01.810 --> 00:10:04.510
And the other thing is
it scales very well,

00:10:04.510 --> 00:10:07.720
but beyond a certain limit, you
need to start creating shards.

00:10:07.720 --> 00:10:10.954
And those are the core problems
that Cloud Firestore solves.

00:10:10.954 --> 00:10:11.870
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Cool.

00:10:11.870 --> 00:10:15.730
So could you tell me more about
where those limits are more

00:10:15.730 --> 00:10:19.120
or less, so our audience can
have any idea of whether they

00:10:19.120 --> 00:10:23.180
should be using the Realtime
Database or Cloud Firestore?

00:10:23.180 --> 00:10:26.320
ALEX DUFETEL: The scalability
limit of the Realtime Database

00:10:26.320 --> 00:10:29.320
is most often expressed as
the number of concurrent users

00:10:29.320 --> 00:10:30.700
that you can have.

00:10:30.700 --> 00:10:34.260
And those are
approximately 100,000.

00:10:34.260 --> 00:10:36.490
We put this as a hard limit.

00:10:36.490 --> 00:10:39.710
You may actually hit some
scalability issues before that.

00:10:39.710 --> 00:10:41.970
But most of the time, this
is what you can reach.

00:10:41.970 --> 00:10:44.054
FRANCESC CAMPOY: 100,000
concurrent users.

00:10:44.054 --> 00:10:45.220
ALEX DUFETEL: That is a lot.

00:10:45.220 --> 00:10:46.910
FRANCESC CAMPOY: That
is a lot of data.

00:10:46.910 --> 00:10:48.620
Yeah, that is.

00:10:48.620 --> 00:10:50.020
That's a big party.

00:10:50.020 --> 00:10:52.600
Let's look a little bit
more about what people

00:10:52.600 --> 00:10:55.300
were doing with the
Realtime Database

00:10:55.300 --> 00:11:00.460
and how that motivated
this Cloud Firestore story.

00:11:00.460 --> 00:11:03.280
You mentioned that the
way you were modeling data

00:11:03.280 --> 00:11:05.802
before was sometimes awkward.

00:11:05.802 --> 00:11:07.510
Can you talk a little
bit more about that

00:11:07.510 --> 00:11:08.830
and how Cloud
Realtime is different?

00:11:08.830 --> 00:11:09.663
ALEX DUFETEL: Right.

00:11:09.663 --> 00:11:12.580
So first, I'd like to start
with some of the things that

00:11:12.580 --> 00:11:14.154
are similar--

00:11:14.154 --> 00:11:15.070
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Sure.

00:11:15.070 --> 00:11:16.861
ALEX DUFETEL: --with
the Realtime Database.

00:11:16.861 --> 00:11:18.850
So first, like the
Realtime Database,

00:11:18.850 --> 00:11:22.440
Cloud Firestore features
rich client SDKs

00:11:22.440 --> 00:11:26.410
that allow you to build directly
from web, iOS and Android.

00:11:26.410 --> 00:11:28.180
And that's really a
big deal, because it

00:11:28.180 --> 00:11:31.840
allows you to access your data
directly from your clients.

00:11:31.840 --> 00:11:34.390
And it basically
allows you to not

00:11:34.390 --> 00:11:36.730
have to build a proxy
layer to do all the access

00:11:36.730 --> 00:11:39.070
control, the data validation,
and the delivery of data

00:11:39.070 --> 00:11:39.569
to your app.

00:11:39.569 --> 00:11:41.590
So it saves you a lot of time.

00:11:41.590 --> 00:11:44.530
This is made possible in the
backend by a set of security

00:11:44.530 --> 00:11:47.200
rules that developers
can define that basically

00:11:47.200 --> 00:11:50.510
allow you to define who and
how your data can be accessed.

00:11:50.510 --> 00:11:54.190
So along the way, this model
of client SDKs with rules

00:11:54.190 --> 00:11:57.310
also makes it much easier
to build more secure apps.

00:11:57.310 --> 00:11:59.800
The second thing that is similar
with the Realtime Database

00:11:59.800 --> 00:12:01.420
is that Cloud Firestore
allows you to fetch

00:12:01.420 --> 00:12:02.378
your data in real-time.

00:12:02.378 --> 00:12:03.220
Time.

00:12:03.220 --> 00:12:05.959
Now this is actually
a very, very big deal.

00:12:05.959 --> 00:12:07.750
On the surface, you
may think that is great

00:12:07.750 --> 00:12:10.540
because it allows you to
build real-time functionality.

00:12:10.540 --> 00:12:13.529
Typically, you want to build
a collaborative whiteboard,

00:12:13.529 --> 00:12:15.070
or you want to build
a messaging app,

00:12:15.070 --> 00:12:18.590
and having a real-time database
for that is really cool.

00:12:18.590 --> 00:12:20.350
While that's true,
the advantages

00:12:20.350 --> 00:12:24.080
of having a real-time database
extend way beyond that.

00:12:24.080 --> 00:12:27.190
In particular, one of the
things it allows you to do

00:12:27.190 --> 00:12:30.130
is to just build much
more reactive apps.

00:12:30.130 --> 00:12:33.430
The other thing is
nowadays, very often,

00:12:33.430 --> 00:12:35.680
you need to sync your data
across potentially multiple

00:12:35.680 --> 00:12:36.580
devices.

00:12:36.580 --> 00:12:39.190
And you get that out of the
box with the Realtime Database.

00:12:39.190 --> 00:12:41.620
MARK MANDEL: Does this also
work, say, offline and online?

00:12:41.620 --> 00:12:44.320
I know Realtime Database had
that as an option as well.

00:12:44.320 --> 00:12:45.695
ALEX DUFETEL:
Right, so actually,

00:12:45.695 --> 00:12:48.380
that is one of the
features that I

00:12:48.380 --> 00:12:51.962
think our group of developers
testing the Cloud Firestore so

00:12:51.962 --> 00:12:53.420
far has been most
excited about it.

00:12:53.420 --> 00:12:54.770
Because it's something
that's very complicated

00:12:54.770 --> 00:12:56.269
to build as a
developer, because you

00:12:56.269 --> 00:12:57.990
need to build all
the sync logic,

00:12:57.990 --> 00:12:59.240
the conflict resolution logic.

00:12:59.240 --> 00:13:02.300
And Firestore does this
completely behind the scenes.

00:13:02.300 --> 00:13:04.550
It does this for
Android, for iOS,

00:13:04.550 --> 00:13:06.800
and now, it does this for
web as well, which is unique

00:13:06.800 --> 00:13:09.230
and wasn't available for
the Realtime Database.

00:13:09.230 --> 00:13:10.813
For those listening,
one of the things

00:13:10.813 --> 00:13:12.470
that is really
powerful with this

00:13:12.470 --> 00:13:14.420
is there's really two
things to offline.

00:13:14.420 --> 00:13:17.270
One is kind of the
vanilla use case, which

00:13:17.270 --> 00:13:20.550
is, I want to use my app
offline purely-- say I'm going

00:13:20.550 --> 00:13:22.220
on the plane, I have no Wi-Fi.

00:13:22.220 --> 00:13:24.380
I want to use my app
for a couple of hours,

00:13:24.380 --> 00:13:28.280
and then when I reconnect,
all my data gets synced back.

00:13:28.280 --> 00:13:30.830
But there's another
aspect which is just as

00:13:30.830 --> 00:13:34.220
important, is what we
call latency compensation.

00:13:34.220 --> 00:13:37.550
And what that is
is basically, we

00:13:37.550 --> 00:13:39.860
switch between
offline and online

00:13:39.860 --> 00:13:42.380
behind the scenes for you
so that we can compensate

00:13:42.380 --> 00:13:43.700
for network flakiness.

00:13:43.700 --> 00:13:46.054
So say you're in the
airport, you have poor Wi-Fi.

00:13:46.054 --> 00:13:47.720
What typically happens
in the normal app

00:13:47.720 --> 00:13:50.480
is your app slows down as
your requests slow down.

00:13:50.480 --> 00:13:52.010
With Cloud Firestore,
what happens

00:13:52.010 --> 00:13:54.710
is that we automatically
serve you what's in the cache,

00:13:54.710 --> 00:14:01.460
or we cache your rights to wait
for your network to be back

00:14:01.460 --> 00:14:02.820
to sync those changes.

00:14:02.820 --> 00:14:06.020
So as a user, your app
continues working beautifully.

00:14:06.020 --> 00:14:07.797
MARK MANDEL: And
that's a new feature?

00:14:07.797 --> 00:14:09.380
ALEX DUFETEL: No,
latency compensation

00:14:09.380 --> 00:14:11.750
was available for the
Realtime Database.

00:14:11.750 --> 00:14:14.160
But now it's available
for web as well.

00:14:14.160 --> 00:14:15.110
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Cool.

00:14:15.110 --> 00:14:19.310
So now it's available for web
as well with Cloud Firestore?

00:14:19.310 --> 00:14:21.080
ALEX DUFETEL: With
Cloud Firestore.

00:14:21.080 --> 00:14:24.830
So just back to the
data model question.

00:14:24.830 --> 00:14:28.250
So Cloud Firestore features
a familiar collection

00:14:28.250 --> 00:14:30.960
and document model.

00:14:30.960 --> 00:14:33.550
And this model is
architected-- it

00:14:33.550 --> 00:14:37.100
has a little bit of a twist over
typical collection documents,

00:14:37.100 --> 00:14:39.300
because it allows you
to very easily structure

00:14:39.300 --> 00:14:40.671
your data hierarchically.

00:14:40.671 --> 00:14:42.170
And the reason why
this is important

00:14:42.170 --> 00:14:45.320
is that it allows you to
tie related data together.

00:14:45.320 --> 00:14:48.800
And, as we've seen, this
is the most intuitive way

00:14:48.800 --> 00:14:49.819
to store your data.

00:14:49.819 --> 00:14:51.860
And what we've learned
with the Realtime Database

00:14:51.860 --> 00:14:54.320
is that it is
extremely important

00:14:54.320 --> 00:14:57.290
that the intuitive
design patterns are also

00:14:57.290 --> 00:14:58.460
the right ones.

00:14:58.460 --> 00:14:59.370
This has two effects.

00:14:59.370 --> 00:15:02.630
One, so it considerably
accelerates the learning curve

00:15:02.630 --> 00:15:04.490
when you're starting
to use the product.

00:15:04.490 --> 00:15:07.580
But even more importantly,
it prevents you

00:15:07.580 --> 00:15:10.420
from having to re-architect
your data as your app grows.

00:15:10.420 --> 00:15:12.420
So it's something that
we're very excited about.

00:15:12.420 --> 00:15:14.336
The second thing that
we're very excited about

00:15:14.336 --> 00:15:18.230
is that this data model comes
with very expressive queries,

00:15:18.230 --> 00:15:20.730
which is really new compared
to the Realtime Database, which

00:15:20.730 --> 00:15:23.390
had a very, very basic
querying language.

00:15:23.390 --> 00:15:26.390
And last but not
least, these queries

00:15:26.390 --> 00:15:28.320
are extremely performant.

00:15:28.320 --> 00:15:31.820
So we've actually architected
the database and our queries

00:15:31.820 --> 00:15:35.600
in a way that all queries
scale with the results set,

00:15:35.600 --> 00:15:36.410
not the data set.

00:15:36.410 --> 00:15:38.909
So what that means that is that
if you're querying, fetching

00:15:38.909 --> 00:15:41.360
one result out of 100,
or out of 100 million,

00:15:41.360 --> 00:15:43.010
you'll get the same performance.

00:15:43.010 --> 00:15:43.460
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Nice.

00:15:43.460 --> 00:15:44.700
MARK MANDEL: That's really nice.

00:15:44.700 --> 00:15:46.658
FRANCESC CAMPOY: So could
you talk a little bit

00:15:46.658 --> 00:15:49.445
about the query capabilities
of both the Realtime

00:15:49.445 --> 00:15:53.420
Database and Cloud Firestore?

00:15:53.420 --> 00:15:55.400
What kind of things can you do?

00:15:55.400 --> 00:15:58.160
ALEX DUFETEL: So typically,
with the Realtime Database,

00:15:58.160 --> 00:16:03.320
you were limited to a
filter on a single property.

00:16:03.320 --> 00:16:06.860
With Cloud Firestore, you can
filter on multiple properties,

00:16:06.860 --> 00:16:11.090
you can mix equalities
and inequality filters.

00:16:11.090 --> 00:16:14.540
You can do offsets, you
can do cursor positions.

00:16:14.540 --> 00:16:16.239
You can do quite a
bit of fancy things.

00:16:16.239 --> 00:16:17.780
MARK MANDEL: Can
you do aggregations?

00:16:17.780 --> 00:16:18.830
Like if you want
to use this report?

00:16:18.830 --> 00:16:20.120
Or is it not really
that kind of tool?

00:16:20.120 --> 00:16:21.765
ALEX DUFETEL: No, you
cannot do aggregations.

00:16:21.765 --> 00:16:22.431
DAN MCGRATH: No.

00:16:22.431 --> 00:16:25.190
You can do small aggregations
on the client side,

00:16:25.190 --> 00:16:27.350
taking into account
the scalability factor

00:16:27.350 --> 00:16:31.384
of returning result set rather
than dataset sides latency.

00:16:31.384 --> 00:16:32.300
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Cool.

00:16:32.300 --> 00:16:34.520
So you mentioned that
when you're doing filters,

00:16:34.520 --> 00:16:37.610
you're doing filters on
properties, among other things.

00:16:37.610 --> 00:16:40.800
Those properties-- is there
a schema for people that

00:16:40.800 --> 00:16:42.190
don't know that much about--

00:16:42.190 --> 00:16:44.420
there's NoSQL, so I'd say no.

00:16:44.420 --> 00:16:47.732
But is there, somewhere, a
list of all the properties?

00:16:47.732 --> 00:16:49.190
DAN MCGRATH: So
like you mentioned,

00:16:49.190 --> 00:16:51.450
it's really a
schema-less database.

00:16:51.450 --> 00:16:53.240
So that means every
document can have

00:16:53.240 --> 00:16:55.340
its own independent schema.

00:16:55.340 --> 00:16:57.920
You can get a list of fields
back from each document

00:16:57.920 --> 00:17:00.680
and query out against them,
but it makes it really easy

00:17:00.680 --> 00:17:04.290
to continually evolve your app
as you add new fields to it.

00:17:04.290 --> 00:17:06.440
You don't need to go back
and alter tables and wait

00:17:06.440 --> 00:17:07.890
for things to rebuild.

00:17:07.890 --> 00:17:08.839
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Cool.

00:17:08.839 --> 00:17:09.713
MARK MANDEL: Awesome.

00:17:09.713 --> 00:17:13.190
So this sounds super cool.

00:17:13.190 --> 00:17:16.390
I'm going to ask the
question as well.

00:17:16.390 --> 00:17:18.230
Apart from the
client side, is there

00:17:18.230 --> 00:17:20.359
ways of interacting with
that on the backend?

00:17:20.359 --> 00:17:22.170
Is there a server
integration part?

00:17:22.170 --> 00:17:23.420
DAN MCGRATH: Yeah, absolutely.

00:17:23.420 --> 00:17:26.450
We actually have a set of
server SDKs you can use,

00:17:26.450 --> 00:17:29.360
whether it's compute VMs,
or a Kubernetes cluster,

00:17:29.360 --> 00:17:32.030
or even [INAUDIBLE]
you're running locally

00:17:32.030 --> 00:17:32.880
on your machine.

00:17:32.880 --> 00:17:37.750
So we've got Java,
Python, Node.js, and Go--

00:17:37.750 --> 00:17:40.294
several client
libraries available.

00:17:40.294 --> 00:17:41.210
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Cool.

00:17:41.210 --> 00:17:43.270
So what are those servr--

00:17:43.270 --> 00:17:45.290
or backend is the
case, useful for?

00:17:45.290 --> 00:17:48.146
What kind of use case have you
seen people using them for?

00:17:48.146 --> 00:17:49.520
DAN MCGRATH: Many
different ones.

00:17:49.520 --> 00:17:51.290
Two quick examples
are integrating

00:17:51.290 --> 00:17:52.360
with existing systems.

00:17:52.360 --> 00:17:53.870
So you're writing
a new mobile app,

00:17:53.870 --> 00:17:56.390
and you want data updates
to be reflected out

00:17:56.390 --> 00:17:57.950
into your mobile app or website.

00:17:57.950 --> 00:18:00.410
You can do that via
those server SDKs.

00:18:00.410 --> 00:18:03.920
The other big one is offline
big-batch processing.

00:18:03.920 --> 00:18:06.470
You want to update something
or add new data into it.

00:18:06.470 --> 00:18:07.650
That's very easy to do.

00:18:07.650 --> 00:18:10.244
Just spin up a compute
VM, and off you go.

00:18:10.244 --> 00:18:11.660
MARK MANDEL: And
since server-less

00:18:11.660 --> 00:18:14.150
is the new hotness, is there
some sort of Cloud Function

00:18:14.150 --> 00:18:15.249
type thing going on?

00:18:15.249 --> 00:18:16.040
ALEX DUFETEL: Sure.

00:18:16.040 --> 00:18:19.070
So Cloud Firestore allows you to
integrate with Cloud Functions,

00:18:19.070 --> 00:18:24.620
allowing you to trigger
code on writes and deletes.

00:18:24.620 --> 00:18:25.579
MARK MANDEL: And edits?

00:18:25.579 --> 00:18:26.578
ALEX DUFETEL: And edits.

00:18:26.578 --> 00:18:27.300
[LAUGHING]

00:18:27.300 --> 00:18:28.480
MARK MANDEL: Excellent.

00:18:28.480 --> 00:18:29.160
Cool.

00:18:29.160 --> 00:18:31.850
So what does the development
experience look like?

00:18:31.850 --> 00:18:35.090
Is this integrated in the
usual Firebase tooling?

00:18:35.090 --> 00:18:39.470
Like If I want to play with
this thing, what am I writing?

00:18:39.470 --> 00:18:42.110
ALEX DUFETEL: So first, if you
want to play with this, head

00:18:42.110 --> 00:18:44.870
to Firebase, create a project.

00:18:44.870 --> 00:18:47.450
And then download--
so you can either

00:18:47.450 --> 00:18:51.650
use it from the client
SDKs, iOS, Android, or web,

00:18:51.650 --> 00:18:53.930
or the server SDKs we
were talking about.

00:18:53.930 --> 00:18:56.990
Just download the
SDKs, load your config,

00:18:56.990 --> 00:18:57.970
and you're ready to go.

00:18:57.970 --> 00:18:58.455
MARK MANDEL: That's it?

00:18:58.455 --> 00:18:59.413
DAN MCGRATH: That's it.

00:18:59.413 --> 00:19:01.100
ALEX DUFETEL: Pretty much, yeah.

00:19:01.100 --> 00:19:01.970
Very easy.

00:19:01.970 --> 00:19:03.511
FRANCESC CAMPOY: So
one of the things

00:19:03.511 --> 00:19:05.600
that I love about
Firebase Realtime

00:19:05.600 --> 00:19:08.120
Database is that
dashboard where you can

00:19:08.120 --> 00:19:09.530
see the data on real-time time.

00:19:09.530 --> 00:19:11.700
And if your program
changes something,

00:19:11.700 --> 00:19:13.435
it just changes
colors and stuff.

00:19:13.435 --> 00:19:14.390
It's amazing.

00:19:14.390 --> 00:19:16.870
And it's, for demos,
the best thing ever.

00:19:16.870 --> 00:19:20.000
Does Cloud Firestore also
support these kind of things?

00:19:20.000 --> 00:19:21.740
ALEX DUFETEL: Yes, it does.

00:19:21.740 --> 00:19:24.460
It is something that we're
also very excited about.

00:19:24.460 --> 00:19:28.290
I think when I first discovered
Firebase many years ago,

00:19:28.290 --> 00:19:30.332
it was actually my
co-founder had discovered it

00:19:30.332 --> 00:19:31.040
over the weekend.

00:19:31.040 --> 00:19:35.540
And he showed me his laptop
with that dashboard and things

00:19:35.540 --> 00:19:36.770
changing in real-time.

00:19:36.770 --> 00:19:39.234
He had created a whiteboard--
so we were in collaboration,

00:19:39.234 --> 00:19:40.400
he had created a whiteboard.

00:19:40.400 --> 00:19:42.470
He showed me how you
could move a square

00:19:42.470 --> 00:19:44.054
around the white
board, and those

00:19:44.054 --> 00:19:46.470
coordinates would update in
real-time and through the data

00:19:46.470 --> 00:19:46.970
viewer.

00:19:46.970 --> 00:19:47.780
And we were hooked.

00:19:47.780 --> 00:19:48.488
And it was funny.

00:19:48.488 --> 00:19:50.030
It was really funny.

00:19:50.030 --> 00:19:53.450
And this was really core to
the experience of the Firebase

00:19:53.450 --> 00:19:55.550
database, and so it
really mattered to us

00:19:55.550 --> 00:19:59.150
that we'd be able to provide a
similar experience with Cloud

00:19:59.150 --> 00:20:00.410
Firestore.

00:20:00.410 --> 00:20:04.010
So we did our best to
do something as cool.

00:20:04.010 --> 00:20:06.735
And we look forward to having
feedback on what we did.

00:20:06.735 --> 00:20:07.610
MARK MANDEL: Awesome.

00:20:07.610 --> 00:20:09.080
So all of this
sounds really great,

00:20:09.080 --> 00:20:10.621
and you talked about
some comparisons

00:20:10.621 --> 00:20:13.760
between the Realtime Database
and the new Cloud Firestore.

00:20:13.760 --> 00:20:15.470
Does this mean if
I'm sitting down,

00:20:15.470 --> 00:20:18.140
I've got a greenfield project,
I'm going to build a thing.

00:20:18.140 --> 00:20:19.750
Should I use Realtime Database?

00:20:19.750 --> 00:20:21.120
Should I use Cloud Firestore?

00:20:21.120 --> 00:20:22.490
Which one should I use?

00:20:22.490 --> 00:20:25.910
ALEX DUFETEL: So we're
recommending for new apps

00:20:25.910 --> 00:20:27.980
to use Cloud Firestore
for developers

00:20:27.980 --> 00:20:29.960
who are comfortable
using a beta product.

00:20:29.960 --> 00:20:32.060
That's the short answer.

00:20:32.060 --> 00:20:36.470
The long answer is you can
use both products together.

00:20:36.470 --> 00:20:39.350
For most use cases,
Cloud Firestore

00:20:39.350 --> 00:20:41.060
will be the best solution.

00:20:41.060 --> 00:20:45.140
For certain edge cases,
the Realtime Database

00:20:45.140 --> 00:20:50.570
might make more sense to
use from a cost-efficiency

00:20:50.570 --> 00:20:51.840
perspective.

00:20:51.840 --> 00:20:53.870
And the reason
for this is that--

00:20:53.870 --> 00:20:56.182
so first of all, the
costs of operating

00:20:56.182 --> 00:20:57.890
Cloud Firestore and
the Realtime Database

00:20:57.890 --> 00:21:01.400
are not the same, because
one is a replicated database,

00:21:01.400 --> 00:21:04.130
and the other is not.

00:21:04.130 --> 00:21:07.920
And so typically, with
the Realtime Database,

00:21:07.920 --> 00:21:09.680
something that is
very, very efficient

00:21:09.680 --> 00:21:15.480
and cost-efficient is to update
small bits of data very fast.

00:21:15.480 --> 00:21:17.660
So say you're building a
collaborative whiteboard,

00:21:17.660 --> 00:21:19.910
and you want to move
a cursor around,

00:21:19.910 --> 00:21:21.950
and you sync that
position, and you

00:21:21.950 --> 00:21:24.200
want to sync that
several times a second.

00:21:24.200 --> 00:21:26.650
This is something that the
Realtime Database is well

00:21:26.650 --> 00:21:30.590
built for, because you're only
charged for outgoing bandwidth.

00:21:30.590 --> 00:21:32.870
So even if you're doing
many, many document writes,

00:21:32.870 --> 00:21:35.420
it's still OK.

00:21:35.420 --> 00:21:38.840
This model with Cloud Firestore
doesn't make as much sense,

00:21:38.840 --> 00:21:42.400
because, first of all,
we're replicating your data

00:21:42.400 --> 00:21:44.480
for additional reliability.

00:21:44.480 --> 00:21:46.580
And that type of data
doesn't make sense

00:21:46.580 --> 00:21:50.120
to be replicated
in that use case.

00:21:50.120 --> 00:21:53.340
And Cloud Firestore
charges per operation,

00:21:53.340 --> 00:21:55.820
so you're charged for every
write, and delete, and edit.

00:21:55.820 --> 00:21:58.535
Which is actually more
intuitive for most use cases,

00:21:58.535 --> 00:22:00.910
but for this use case, for
example, it's less performant.

00:22:00.910 --> 00:22:03.410
So in this type of
scenario, what we're telling

00:22:03.410 --> 00:22:07.260
users is it's perfectly fine
to use both databases together.

00:22:07.260 --> 00:22:09.590
The other thing
is both databases

00:22:09.590 --> 00:22:14.360
will live alongside in
the Firebase console.

00:22:14.360 --> 00:22:16.750
And if you're using the
Realtime Database today,

00:22:16.750 --> 00:22:18.944
it is totally fine
to continue using it.

00:22:18.944 --> 00:22:20.360
We'll be supporting
and developing

00:22:20.360 --> 00:22:21.884
both products going forward.

00:22:21.884 --> 00:22:22.800
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Cool.

00:22:22.800 --> 00:22:25.750
So when you say that you can use
both of them at the same time,

00:22:25.750 --> 00:22:28.410
that doesn't mean that the
data is replicating to both.

00:22:28.410 --> 00:22:30.590
It's that you have two
different databases,

00:22:30.590 --> 00:22:32.290
and you should
choose either one.

00:22:32.290 --> 00:22:33.290
ALEX DUFETEL: That's it.

00:22:33.290 --> 00:22:35.780
You can also use Cloud Functions
to replicate data from one

00:22:35.780 --> 00:22:37.645
place to another very easily.

00:22:37.645 --> 00:22:39.050
FRANCESC CAMPOY: So you
mentioned real quick something

00:22:39.050 --> 00:22:41.174
that is actually really
important for our audience,

00:22:41.174 --> 00:22:42.310
which is data replication.

00:22:42.310 --> 00:22:44.140
Can you tell us a little
bit more about it?

00:22:44.140 --> 00:22:45.410
DAN MCGRATH: Yeah,
I'll take that one.

00:22:45.410 --> 00:22:47.160
Alex mentioned it's a
replicated database,

00:22:47.160 --> 00:22:48.950
so I'll tell you a
little bit about how

00:22:48.950 --> 00:22:50.810
it works under the covers.

00:22:50.810 --> 00:22:54.440
It's what we call a multi-region
replicated database.

00:22:54.440 --> 00:22:56.990
And that means whenever
you write data,

00:22:56.990 --> 00:23:00.440
we're committing it not just
to one or two data centers.

00:23:00.440 --> 00:23:02.210
We're committing
it to multiple data

00:23:02.210 --> 00:23:05.670
centers across distinct
geographical regions.

00:23:05.670 --> 00:23:09.860
So when you commit data, it
actually is durable, even

00:23:09.860 --> 00:23:11.750
in the face of disasters.

00:23:11.750 --> 00:23:14.360
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Does that
mean that those replicas also

00:23:14.360 --> 00:23:15.230
cam take the reads?

00:23:15.230 --> 00:23:18.890
So if your audiences,
or your user base

00:23:18.890 --> 00:23:23.630
is distributed geographically,
they will get the fastest?

00:23:23.630 --> 00:23:25.880
DAN MCGRATH: Yeah, I think
probably the most important

00:23:25.880 --> 00:23:30.560
thing to note first off is Cloud
Firestore goes across Google's

00:23:30.560 --> 00:23:32.730
internal network.

00:23:32.730 --> 00:23:35.930
So that means we're going
to in-bound your request

00:23:35.930 --> 00:23:37.640
from the closest
point of presence,

00:23:37.640 --> 00:23:39.650
regardless of what
country you're in,

00:23:39.650 --> 00:23:42.370
and use our fiberoptics to get
you to the right data center

00:23:42.370 --> 00:23:43.580
to serve data.

00:23:43.580 --> 00:23:46.310
Each data center
that we replicate

00:23:46.310 --> 00:23:49.402
to can serve data out of it.

00:23:49.402 --> 00:23:50.860
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
What about rights?

00:23:50.860 --> 00:23:54.770
Basically, my question
is, is it master, active--

00:23:54.770 --> 00:23:57.410
all of them are active and
can take the reads and writes?

00:23:57.410 --> 00:23:59.910
Or do you always need to go
to one, and the other one,

00:23:59.910 --> 00:24:01.100
it just reads replicas?

00:24:01.100 --> 00:24:04.400
DAN MCGRATH: Yeah, so we are in
an active-active configuration.

00:24:04.400 --> 00:24:06.950
Our system will
automatically route

00:24:06.950 --> 00:24:08.660
to where we think
the quickest is going

00:24:08.660 --> 00:24:10.834
to be to start with, though.

00:24:10.834 --> 00:24:11.750
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Nice.

00:24:11.750 --> 00:24:15.050
Do you get to configure
how many replicas you get?

00:24:15.050 --> 00:24:17.170
Or is that a fixed
number that is

00:24:17.170 --> 00:24:18.890
the safest bet, and that's it?

00:24:18.890 --> 00:24:21.440
DAN MCGRATH: So this is a fully
managed solution by Google.

00:24:21.440 --> 00:24:23.900
That means Google engineering
takes care of everything

00:24:23.900 --> 00:24:27.350
for you-- node count, RAM
allocation, cluster management.

00:24:27.350 --> 00:24:29.240
It's all handled
by us, so you just

00:24:29.240 --> 00:24:32.357
need to think about
how you use the SDK.

00:24:32.357 --> 00:24:34.190
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Mark
was mentioning before,

00:24:34.190 --> 00:24:36.954
if I'm to build
something from scratch,

00:24:36.954 --> 00:24:38.120
which one should I be using?

00:24:41.420 --> 00:24:43.810
I'm already using
Realtime Database, right?

00:24:43.810 --> 00:24:45.710
Firebase Realtime Database.

00:24:45.710 --> 00:24:47.600
Under what conditions
would you say

00:24:47.600 --> 00:24:50.525
that Cloud Firestore
is something

00:24:50.525 --> 00:24:53.510
that I should
consider moving to?

00:24:53.510 --> 00:24:56.220
ALEX DUFETEL: So there's
a couple of, I guess,

00:24:56.220 --> 00:24:58.890
scenarios where it makes sense.

00:24:58.890 --> 00:25:04.010
One is, if you want to
implement new functionality that

00:25:04.010 --> 00:25:06.740
will require some
complex querying,

00:25:06.740 --> 00:25:09.470
then definitely give
Cloud Firestore a try.

00:25:09.470 --> 00:25:12.920
Also, if you're really
interested in getting

00:25:12.920 --> 00:25:17.390
the benefits of the
multi-regional data sync,

00:25:17.390 --> 00:25:20.210
then definitely use
Cloud Firestore.

00:25:20.210 --> 00:25:23.690
And again, you can
use both together.

00:25:23.690 --> 00:25:27.070
So what we've been
seeing with our testers

00:25:27.070 --> 00:25:31.550
so far, especially those who
are using the Realtime Database,

00:25:31.550 --> 00:25:35.910
is that you can typically
just implement a new feature,

00:25:35.910 --> 00:25:38.816
have some of that data
live in Cloud Firestore

00:25:38.816 --> 00:25:40.190
and use Cloud
Firestore for that.

00:25:40.190 --> 00:25:41.510
MARK MANDEL: And it also
sounds like there's some web

00:25:41.510 --> 00:25:43.504
capabilities that you
wouldn't necessarily

00:25:43.504 --> 00:25:45.170
