00:30:06.160 --> 00:30:08.660
So it's part of the
API, which you can also

00:30:08.660 --> 00:30:10.470
use from GCloud directly.

00:30:10.470 --> 00:30:13.280
So super simple,
and you end up being

00:30:13.280 --> 00:30:16.820
able to download all the
contents of your Datastore.

00:30:16.820 --> 00:30:18.590
So then you can upload
it to other places,

00:30:18.590 --> 00:30:20.720
like let's say you want
to upload it to BigQuery

00:30:20.720 --> 00:30:23.270
or whatever-- very
simple to do from there.

00:30:23.270 --> 00:30:26.180
You can also upload
into Datastore.

00:30:26.180 --> 00:30:27.860
And I know that some
people want to do

00:30:27.860 --> 00:30:29.600
these kind of things for QA.

00:30:29.600 --> 00:30:31.920
So you have a golden
image or something

00:30:31.920 --> 00:30:34.310
like that, where you
get that file, upload it

00:30:34.310 --> 00:30:36.650
to the Datastore, and then
you start running from there.

00:30:36.650 --> 00:30:37.040
MARK MANDEL: This is nice.

00:30:37.040 --> 00:30:38.330
I'm actually having a
look through as well,

00:30:38.330 --> 00:30:40.100
and I'm seeing that there is a--

00:30:40.100 --> 00:30:43.550
it looks like IM permissions
as well to hook in here so

00:30:43.550 --> 00:30:46.970
that you can dictate who or
even what services could then

00:30:46.970 --> 00:30:48.729
be able to import and export.

00:30:48.729 --> 00:30:51.020
So people who shouldn't be
able to just go, oh, look, I

00:30:51.020 --> 00:30:52.947
can import into
production, that's fine.

00:30:52.947 --> 00:30:54.530
You'd have a lot of
control over this.

00:30:54.530 --> 00:30:56.425
This looks actually really nice.

00:30:56.425 --> 00:30:57.800
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
The only warning

00:30:57.800 --> 00:30:59.390
that I would give
to anyone doing

00:30:59.390 --> 00:31:02.210
this is when you
are doing a backup,

00:31:02.210 --> 00:31:05.300
you will be charged the price
of reading those entities.

00:31:05.300 --> 00:31:07.182
So it's something
to keep in mind.

00:31:07.182 --> 00:31:09.140
I mean, it makes sense,
because you're actually

00:31:09.140 --> 00:31:10.650
reading those entities.

00:31:10.650 --> 00:31:13.260
But keep in mind that if
you have a huge database,

00:31:13.260 --> 00:31:16.790
and you're taking backups every
hour, that might be expensive.

00:31:16.790 --> 00:31:19.210
So take that into account
and be careful with that.

